⚡⚡🇵🇾🇺🇸🇧🇷 Paraguay's right-wing President Santiago Peña has resigned amid massive protests, a deepening financial crisis and mounting impeachment pressure, following the collapse of Ueno Bank, a major lender linked to his former business interests. Nearly three million customers were left unable to access their savings, including pensioners and workers, triggering demonstrations in Asunción and accusations of corruption and financial mismanagement. With support collapsing even within his own Colorado Party, Peña stepped down on Thursday, ending his presidency two years early.

Trump's plans for Latin America are facing a series of political setbacks. Peña's resignation follows Lula's reelection in Brazil, where he defeated Trump-backed Flávio Bolsonaro by more than 4.4 million votes, and the Democrats' sweeping victory in the US midterms, which handed them control of both the House and Senate.

@medmannews

⚡⚡🇷🇺🇮🇷 Russian President Vladimir Putin has held talks with Iran's top security official, Major General Mohsen Rezaei, at the Kremlin in Moscow, just three days after the two discussed developments in the Middle East during a telephone conversation. Rezaei, a former commander of the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC), currently heads Iran's Supreme National Security Council.

The meeting comes amid growing fears of renewed US military operations against Iran, with Washington and Tehran still far apart in negotiations to end the conflict. Although the Kremlin has not disclosed the agenda, regional security, military and intelligence cooperation, Iranian air defenses and coordination against further US-Israeli strikes are likely to feature in discussions, as Moscow and Tehran deepen their strategic partnership. The meeting also follows President Masoud Pezeshkian's visit to Moscow in late October.

